{
  "texts": [
    {
      "content": "Maxkodia Flora",
      "left": "726px",
      "top": "60px",
      "scale": "2.8000000000000016",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "gobold",
      "fontSize": "28px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "In Maxkodia, the term “plant” is a misleading simplification.\nSpecies classified as flora may exhibit photosynthetic structures,\nroot systems, and modular growth, but many display traits more\n akin to hybrid, symbiotic, or even predatory organisms.",
      "left": "444.3px",
      "top": "232.2px",
      "scale": "1.6000000000000005",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "22px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "Their physiology defies conventional taxonomy, blending protein-rich tissues, active\nsensory systems, and electromagnetic responsesto environmental stimuli. These entities are not\npassive or static: some hunt, others communicate, and many interact with their\nsurroundings through pulses, pheromones, or radiation.",
      "left": "720.8px",
      "top": "603.2px",
      "scale": "1.6000000000000005",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "22px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "Their ecological role extends far beyond oxygen production or nutrient cycling. They function as living nodes,\naware of their space, capable of modifying and defending it. In certain cases, their behavior\nresembles that of social organisms, with patterns of care, territoriality, and coordinated defense.",
      "left": "232px",
      "top": "980.6px",
      "scale": "1.6000000000000005",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "22px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "In others, their presence alters the chemistry of the environment, generating zones of exclusion or\nritual attraction. This functional ambiguity has led to the need for an alternative\nclassification system, one based not on morphology but on ecological interaction and behavioral complexity.",
      "left": "665.8px",
      "top": "1574.4px",
      "scale": "1.4000000000000004",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "22px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "These species exhibit behavioral patterns that suggest a form of distributed environmental awareness.\nSome respond to non-local stimuli, while others appear to anticipate climatic shifts or migratory events,\nas if part of a broader sensory network that transcends individual biology.",
      "left": "523.6px",
      "top": "2062.2px",
      "scale": "1.6000000000000005",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "22px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "This sensitivity, still poorly understood, raises questions about the very nature of plant life\nin Maxkodia: are they singular organisms, or extensions of a larger ecosystemic intelligence? ",
      "left": "204px",
      "top": "2430.8px",
      "scale": "1.4000000000000004",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "22px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "The boundary between plant, creature, and system becomes increasingly blurred,\nalong with the need to redefine what constitutes life in more fluid and contextual terms.",
      "left": "665.4px",
      "top": "2761px",
      "scale": "1.8000000000000007",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "22px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    }
  ],
